ue" :db/id 17592186364956} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2581fc-f5d1-56c8-ab61-5d417f0d30e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186364957} {:answer/id #uuid "14d9082f-208d-5d34-9c63-524f45f1904f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364958} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c099fe-52bc-5e28-83a9-4aad7f968450" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364959} {:answer/id #uuid "df4cf5da-869a-50b7-a8cd-35b980cbe9eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186364960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9c099fe-52bc-5e28-83a9-4aad7f968450" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364959} :answer-field/id #uuid "b43375d0-11ca-5e43-8be7-2100aee7f584" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0d9bc49-c94e-5174-9068-2abf75c42684" :question/math-academy-id "q-128490" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\n- $det(M)=10$\n- $det(M)=-5$\n- $M$ is a singular matrix\n\n \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 4\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(12-15)-0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+9\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186060454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f09ed10-b680-5e9c-99e7-04c22b6a5202" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361046} {:answer/id #uuid "4a66f65a-11a7-5f42-83a1-b14b35b1c089" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361158} {:answer/id #uuid "51594ef1-0fed-5155-b685-7f55eca2a956" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361194} {:answer/id #uuid "86cefcfa-29d4-56e3-b026-a13b84142878" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361439} {:answer/id #uuid "be424c64-a1cc-5c27-9b4e-5599f2b8e337" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186361690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a66f65a-11a7-5f42-83a1-b14b35b1c089" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361158} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c65d0d4-2f35-53f8-943b-a9a971d3e887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4279505-241a-54e3-b9b3-ff9b46d09cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-9838" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\n- $det(F)=0$\n- $F$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(F)=-2$\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-1(24-30)+2(21-24) \\\\  & =0-1\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186069366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7e85ee-6515-5fb8-a5a2-074cbc9108df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186327048} {:answer/id #uuid "29de8dce-e7c7-5fc2-94a7-5179e0c2f602" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186327294} {:answer/id #uuid "440c0fb1-d639-5470-acec-fe52787433f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186327530} {:answer/id #uuid "937287c6-1b15-5c81-835f-d6dddc1cd449" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186328220} {:answer/id #uuid "fe29cc74-1bf5-5fbc-b9ad-88af91611a92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186329166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7e85ee-6515-5fb8-a5a2-074cbc9108df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186327048} :answer-field/id #uuid "62261799-63f8-547f-a1a7-46cbd5868baa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "544abde9-c223-5348-b980-33df85581d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48042" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite\n- $f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id 17592186069367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a26128fd-a27e-5a35-8575-639ff9311589" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186067373} {:answer/id #uuid "b3835136-311e-52c3-8f9d-f243c85a4875" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186067374} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f192a9-8305-5e68-97be-10e4dac05d62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186067375} {:answer/id #uuid "330a48ae-091d-566c-827d-bd6e6174a48a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186067376} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7c3791-ee87-5538-8024-8ad1d642d14a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186067377}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1a24ceb9-b141-5695-8ef8-3816685f8054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186067372}] :question/id #uuid "a302c934-1e7e-57a4-882d-8cd125952f61" :question/math-academy-id "q-48064" :question/problem "![](images/02/0218a1e13a0504b20e802100f9e15877beeb2b565a49fe7cd01cf2a0cf0197ee.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x = 2$\n2. $lim_(x → 2)f(x)$ exists\n3. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 2$"}] [{:db/id 17592186088138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2b1967-4af5-520c-811d-5f2fb33cc469" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186302736} {:answer/id #uuid "7b84a815-9470-55cc-8164-a6fb81f55251" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186303223} {:answer/id #uuid "e562f968-7867-523a-a569-53df8d43088c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186303880} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d4c2cd-7975-5587-badc-859966cb0b02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303891} {:answer/id #uuid "ebebf99c-d5e5-53b4-97ef-d68b99aecdfa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186303933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e562f968-7867-523a-a569-53df8d43088c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186303880} :answer-field/id #uuid "3326193e-0a0c-59c1-ad04-260c0af220a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "591724df-60a1-5a9d-9f71-6942e86af15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331601" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=4$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=4.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $4$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186088139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecfc9ad-0591-5d56-b725-3982bf5bcba9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186302848} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c5df2b-0e09-5631-978d-a7638ab3eb56" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303481} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d10eb5-5969-5f1c-9bba-1b1eb48631dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303605} {:answer/id #uuid "e7884954-9537-5ded-91a1-1196e28f05f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186303895} {:answer/id #uuid "ec041024-752a-551d-bc1a-6d7c54f985c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186303934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ecfc9ad-0591-5d56-b725-3982bf5bcba9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186302848} :answer-field/id #uuid "f764d5e3-0408-5d5f-b201-353145c06dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7723c02-822f-5a83-b3af-429d938426d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-331744" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186088275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f45224a-238a-5111-92de-58c0b8134e44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186086305} {:answer/id #uuid "ed48c8d8-11b4-5f0e-a507-091824ef3aa9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186086306} {:answer/id #uuid "09759925-78bc-5d97-9535-7c617e0bf976" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$