res how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186241082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "293fea52-e929-5811-afed-af97ddebf14a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238420} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc29659-6ed2-5300-9382-238c0d2c4601" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186239277} {:answer/id #uuid "9d34961c-bb25-5812-86f9-33a7d799bc19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186239424} {:answer/id #uuid "c71effce-e296-5a41-bbf5-5519f9760ea1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186239809} {:answer/id #uuid "f28361df-fd6a-50c5-8758-cfb0d1e00173" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186240195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "293fea52-e929-5811-afed-af97ddebf14a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238420} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bea141b-d468-560c-9266-a844ba797d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2be15a89-9d36-55d5-9fa3-9993a8ff3b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105578" :question/problem "![](images/33/33f0353e3856fb2b458d17ed1959918e6a3dd96fd0a966d971575ffc212402f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors increase as we move horizontally and vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186241106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e57f851-cf3f-55d4-b6be-245074197a0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186238179} {:answer/id #uuid "7df0fc8d-f369-5515-8dd0-ace7e700bb71" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186239149} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cda894-ceda-51a8-8065-1c4f43584d91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186239797} {:answer/id #uuid "d6768cb9-87b9-5e7b-a6a9-12f7fcc8710a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239929} {:answer/id #uuid "f78ce625-4ae6-52d5-838c-e9371ad41674" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186240244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6768cb9-87b9-5e7b-a6a9-12f7fcc8710a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239929} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6affe32-6ffe-5e4a-9737-d7047243c7ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7d9f8f8-5aac-59da-a7d3-267484c84687" :question/math-academy-id "q-105667" :question/problem "![](images/77/773ff9d880deed92f8034d6949cfee85edbcdf735c8e125f1ec5762137fadb9c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/4e/4e344a0a1f6b0c535c5ba08ee8c87914cf5e7009600f45732023720048b4c493.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statements IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186241108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176e6b67-52d3-5d93-b66b-069cdee86520" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186238257} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf364cd-83cb-56e2-a131-b83254f7db14" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186238452} {:answer/id #uuid "49657e38-b3e1-5210-81e9-349ff603d055" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186238693} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdb155e-0fc6-579e-9b06-82febc84665a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238892} {:answer/id #uuid "6c31e118-15dd-5a06-b2df-5dc22bae417c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186238986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49657e38-b3e1-5210-81e9-349ff603d055" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186238693} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcea847e-0d5c-5ba8-a276-bb66df6bad46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c2154fc-c947-5ba9-b169-f98f4fea25a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-105669" :question/problem "![](images/24/2414de6e2129d4fceaae304d16f9be0c91a545e5a8544b2033f353ddb2e581b9.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186241110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122217e5-263b-5c2f-a1a6-72cd3fe09ec9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186238207} {:answer/id #uuid "1d352e77-efb7-5062-9266-9d12f3ebc8ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186238301} {:answer/id #uuid "27dca027-745d-5b20-9ded-c32694cd03a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186238400} {:answer/id #uuid "6a625d4b-15d9-5410-ba57-4b7cb980b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186238978} {:answer/